## How ObjectMapper uses DBus Signals

For the use-case of discovering object paths and DBus names which have certain interfaces, the object mapper uses 'InterfacesAdded' and 'InterfacesRemoved' to update its internal data structures.

This is a link to ObjectMapper main() function:

https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-objmgr/blob/2a1ef0138c33036c82793104287e72d41f778108/src/main.cpp#L466

Changing a property in aserver instance can result in ```PropertiesChanged``` signal.


## Why i believe the change is needed

Depending on how an application comes to know about an interface on an object path, it may or may not require those signals.

To be notified of Progress and Activation state change, i would assume a ```PropertiesChanged``` signal is required. I did not read the bmcweb implementation, but that would make sense to me.
The ```PropertiesChanged``` signal should be emitted as a result of 


```
version->version(...);
```

since it has a template argument EmitSignal which defaults to true, which you kindly pointed out to me 😊

```
      template <bool EmitSignal = true, typename Arg = std::string>
      void version(Arg&& new_value)
          requires server_details::has_set_property_nomsg<version_t, Instance,
                                                          std::string>
      {
          bool changed = static_cast<Instance*>(this)->set_property(
              version_t{}, std::forward<Arg>(new_value));

          if (changed && EmitSignal)
          {
              _xyz_openbmc_project_software_Version_interface.property_changed("Version");
          }
      }
``` 

So from my perspective, since the code is working with respect to Activation and ActivationProgress, we have emitted the required signals for that.

I am not aware if there is any policy which says that every application must always emit ```InterfacesAdded``` for each interface. This seems to be something optional in dbus.

## Looking at the Code for creating async server instance, my perspective

To emit the ```InterfacesAdded``` signal, it could either happen in the constructor or via some function call. The ObjectManager is documented to have some methods to emit those signals, so i assume they are not always emitted by default.

https://dbus.freedesktop.org/doc/dbus-specification.html#basic-types

Which is confirmed through my experience, that the signals were sometimes not emitted. So it has to be something which needs to be done in the process which is exposing these interfaces.

The first place to look for ```InterfacesAdded``` would be the constructor:


```
version = std::make_unique<SoftwareVersion>(ctx, objectPath.str.c_str());
```

```
  template <typename Instance, typename Server = void>
A>struct Version :
      public std::conditional_t<
          std::is_void_v<Server>,
          sdbusplus::async::server_t<Instance, details::Version>,
          details::Version<Instance, Server>>
  {
      template <typename... Args>
      Version(Args&&... args) :
          std::conditional_t<
              std::is_void_v<Server>,
              sdbusplus::async::server_t<Instance, details::Version>,
              details::Version<Instance, Server>>(std::forward<Args>(args)...)
      {}  
  };
```
with Instance = Software, Server = void.
The template should resolve to 
```
sdbusplus::async::server_t<Instance, details::Version>(std::forward<Args>(args)...)
<=>
sdbusplus::async::server_t<Software, details::Version>(ctx, objectPath.str.c_str())
```
then
```
template <typename Instance, template <typename, typename> typename... Types>
using server_t = server::server<Instance, Types...>;
<=>
server::server<Software, details::Version>(ctx, objectPath.str.c_str())
```

which then leads to this class
```

  template <typename Instance, template <typename, typename> typename... Types>
A>class server :
      public sdbusplus::async::context_ref,
      public Types<Instance, server<Instance, Types...>>...
  { 
    public:
      using Self = server<Instance, Types...>;
      friend details::server_context_friend;

      server() = delete;
      explicit server(sdbusplus::async::context& ctx, const char* path) :
          context_ref(ctx), Types<Instance, Self>(path)...
      {}
  };
```

remembering we passed ```details::Version``` earlier, it will be constructed

```

  template <typename Instance, typename Server>
A>class Version :
      public sdbusplus::common::xyz::openbmc_project::software::Version,
      protected server_details::server_context_friend
  {
    public:
      explicit Version(const char* path) :
          _xyz_openbmc_project_software_Version_interface(
              _context(), path, interface, _vtable, this)
      {}


      /** @brief Emit interface added */
      void emit_added()
      {
          _xyz_openbmc_project_software_Version_interface.emit_added();
      }

      /** @brief Emit interface removed */
      void emit_removed()
      {
          _xyz_openbmc_project_software_Version_interface.emit_removed();
      } 
```

so we are constructing
```

      sdbusplus::server::interface_t
          _xyz_openbmc_project_software_Version_interface;

using interface_t = interface::interface;
```

which then ends up at
```

interface::interface(sdbusplus::bus_t& bus, const char* path,
                     const char* interf, const sdbusplus::vtable_t* vtable,
                     void* context) :
    _bus(get_busp(bus), bus.getInterface()), _path(path), _interf(interf),
    _interface_added(false),
    _slot(makeObjVtable(_bus.getInterface(), get_busp(_bus), _path.c_str(),
                        _interf.c_str(), vtable, context))
{}
```

where our ```_interface_added``` is initialized as ```false```.


Aww shoot! So our constructor did probably not initialize the interface....

Since our instance inherits from ```details::Version``` we can just call 
```emit_added()``` to fix that.

```

      /** @brief Emit interface added */
      void emit_added()
      { 
          _xyz_openbmc_project_software_Version_interface.emit_added();
      } 
```

and there we meet the guard again, to prevent us from emitting this signal more than once.

```
      /** @brief Emit the interface is added on D-Bus */
A>    void emit_added()
      {   
          if (!_interface_added)
          {   
              _bus.emit_interfaces_added(_path.c_str(), {_interf});
              _interface_added = true;
          }   
      }   
```

## Summary

Based on my limited understanding of the code:

- there is a guard in place to prevent us from emitting ```InterfacesAdded``` more than once
- emit_added() should be safe to call if all properties are initialized

i would recommend to call ```emit_added()``` in those cases where we actually need the signal.

There is a change currently in review for sdbusplus which proposes some way to pass properties into aserver constructor so that the constructor will be able to call ```emit_added()``` by itself.


Let me know if you have a different understanding of the code or believe this signal to be generated elsewhere 👍

